```

`new_string`:
```
  - template: 'the service answers "{reply}"'
    block: then
    means: The MCP service's reply to the message is exactly the quoted text.
    terms: [mcp service, reply]
  - template: 'the service answers within {budget:number} milliseconds'
    block: then
    means: >-
      The MCP service's reply to the message the When step sent arrives, and
      the round trip the sender observes — from the moment that message is
      sent until its reply arrives back at the sender — is at most {budget}
      milliseconds. A reply arriving at exactly {budget} milliseconds passes
      (elapsed <= {budget}); no reply arriving at all fails this step.
    terms: [mcp service, reply]
```

## Why this entry, and why it stays unambiguous
- `{budget:number}` is the only slot: the number becomes reusable, while `milliseconds` stays literal and plural, as decided. A later Scenario that needs seconds gets its own entry rather than a unit slot here.
- It cannot collide with `the service answers "{reply}"`: that template requires literal double quotes right after `answers`, which this step does not have, and this template requires the literal word `within` plus digits plus ` milliseconds`, which `the service answers "pong"` does not have.
- It cannot collide with `the mcp service is running` (given) or `the {actor:role} sends "{message}"` (when): different literal text, and different blocks.
- After the change, every step of E2E-001 matches exactly one template in the right block: `the mcp service is running` → given entry; `the Operator sends "ping"` → when entry (`Operator` is a declared `roles[].name`); `the service answers "pong"` → then entry; `the service answers within 200 milliseconds` → the new then entry.

